{"news_id": "news-832b4e609075ae62", "news_state": {"source_qualified": true, "relevance_ready": true, "analysis_ready": true, "translation_complete": true, "translation_status": "complete", "display_ready": true, "wechat_ready": true, "policy_version": "v15.2-independent-news-readiness-1"}, "title": "Tick alert near livestock farms as SFTS fatality rate hits 18.5%", "analysis": {"analysis": {"time": "Published: July 29, 2026 - 11:00:00. Event timeframe: 2013-2024 for reported cases, with heightened risk during summer months when tick activity peaks.", "location_and_population": "South Korea, specifically near livestock barns and pastures. Affected population includes livestock farmers and workers in outdoor environments where ticks are prevalent.", "event": "Confirmed public health alert regarding Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome (SFTS) transmitted by ticks. The Animal and Plant Quarantine Agency issued prevention guidelines for workers near livestock areas.", "scale_impact_and_risk": "2,065 SFTS cases reported in South Korea between 2013 and 2024, resulting in 381 deaths (cumulative fatality rate of 18.5%). Livestock farms are particularly vulnerable due to proximity to tick habitats. No SFTS virus genetic material detected in ticks collected so far in 2024, but 28, 32, and 5 positive tick samples were found in 2023, 2024, and 2023 respectively.", "response_status_and_uncertainty": "Active surveillance being conducted at 76 sites near livestock farms since 2023. Prevention measures include wearing protective clothing, using tick repellents, and regular grass clearing. No approved vaccine or treatment available. Agency is stepping up regional surveillance during peak summer tick activity. Source limitation: Full original article body was reviewed."}, "brief_en": "South Korea is issuing heightened tick alerts for livestock workers as summer temperatures increase tick activity. The Animal and Plant Quarantine Agency has urged workers near barns and pastures to wear protective clothing and use repellents following confirmed cases of Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome (SFTS). Between 2013-2024, 2,065 cases resulted in 381 deaths (18.5% fatality rate). While no SFTS virus has been detected in ticks this year, surveillance continues at 76 sites with 65 positive samples found in recent years. The disease, transmitted through tick bites, causes high fever, vomiting, and diarrhea in both humans and livestock. Prevention remains critical as no vaccine or treatment exists.", "evidence_ids": {"time": ["N1", "N8", "N20"], "location_and_population": ["N3", "N4", "N10"], "event": ["N3", "N6", "N7", "N8"], "scale_impact_and_risk": ["N8", "N19", "N21"], "response_status_and_uncertainty": ["N3", "N9", "N18", "N20", "N22"]}, "source_assessment": "reputable_media", "confidence": "high", "postprocess_audit": {"removed_duplicate_sentences": 0, "repaired_incomplete_fields": [], "evidence_replacements": [], "policy": "v10-exclusive-rhetorical-fields-1"}, "summary_en": "South Korea is issuing heightened tick alerts for livestock workers as summer temperatures increase tick activity. The Animal and Plant Quarantine Agency has urged workers near barns and pastures to wear protective clothing and use repellents following confirmed cases of Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome (SFTS). Between 2013-2024, 2,065 cases resulted in 381 deaths (18.5% fatality rate). While no SFTS virus has been detected in ticks this year, surveillance continues at 76 sites with 65 positive samples found in recent years. The disease, transmitted through tick bites, causes high fever, vomiting, and diarrhea in both humans and livestock. Prevention remains critical as no vaccine or treatment exists.", "language_contract": {"policy_version": "v17.1-source-language-safe-english-1", "source_language": "en", "verified_english": true, "replacements": [], "original_evidence_preserved": false}, "brief_generation": "llm_from_verified_body", "status": "passed",